WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.687
PostgreSQL JSONB lets a relational table store and query semi-structured JSON data.

00:00:03.687 --> 00:00:10.792
In the clinical notes table, the note column contains keys such as symptom, severity, and follow up.

00:00:10.792 --> 00:00:13.937
The arrow with two greater-than symbols extracts a JSON value as text.

00:00:13.937 --> 00:00:19.032
If the value needs to be compared as a Boolean or number, we may cast that text to the appropriate type.

00:00:19.032 --> 00:00:26.292
Run the example and inspect the symptom and severity columns that are extracted from the JSON document.

00:00:26.292 --> 00:00:29.107
The original JSONB column still exists as one stored value.

00:00:29.107 --> 00:00:31.692
The query is simply projecting selected keys into regular result columns.

00:00:31.692 --> 00:00:35.302
JSONB is useful when part of the data is flexible,

00:00:35.302 --> 00:00:40.677
but important relational identifiers and relationships should still be modeled deliberately.

00:00:40.677 --> 00:00:44.722
JSONB is useful when part of a record is genuinely flexible, such as an API payload or optional metadata.

00:00:44.722 --> 00:00:51.302
It should not become an excuse to hide well-understood relational fields inside one large document.

00:00:51.302 --> 00:00:56.292
A strong design keeps stable, frequently queried attributes relational and uses JSONB where flexibility adds

00:00:56.292 --> 00:00:57.280
real value.
